'''Kiso Prefecture''' ({{lang|ja|木曽県}}, ''Kiso-ken'') is a prefecture of [[Okaiken]] located on the eastern highlands of the island of [[Ishijima]], facing the [[Shendan Ocean]] to the east and bordered by [[Haruno Prefecture|Haruno]] to the north, [[Shimabara Prefecture|Shimabara]] to the west, and [[Tachibara Prefecture|Tachibara]] to the south. Its population as of 2026 was 868,918, with approximately 40% concentrated in the prefectural capital of [[Kiso, Kiso|Kiso]] (pop. 378,297). Kiso is one of the most sparsely populated prefectures on Ishijima, a reflection of its rugged, heavily forested terrain and its distance from the industrial coastal corridors.

Kiso is defined by its forests. The {{nihongo|'''Kiso Cypress Forest'''|木曽檜林|Kiso-hinokibayashi}}—a vast expanse of ancient ''hinoki'' cypress and ''sawara'' false cypress that covers over 65% of the prefecture's land area—is one of the most celebrated forest landscapes in Okaiken and the source of the nation's finest construction timber. The forest has shaped every aspect of Kiso's history, economy, and culture: the {{nihongo|'''Kiso Lacquerware'''|木曽漆器|Kiso-shikki}} tradition, one of the three great lacquerware schools of Ishijima, was born from the forest's abundant ''urushi'' trees, and the {{nihongo|'''Kiso Kaidō'''|木曽街道|Kiso-kaidō}}, the historic mountain highway that traverses the prefecture, was built to carry the forest's timber and craft products to the coast.

== History ==

=== Ancient and pre-shogunate period ===
The Kiso highlands were originally inhabited by [[Atsu]] forest communities who developed a sophisticated woodworking culture using the abundant ''hinoki'' cypress and ''sawara'' cypress of the Kiso Forest. The Atsu communities of the Kiso valleys were among the most skilled woodworkers in pre-shogunate Ishijima, producing temple columns, shrine fittings, and the distinctive {{nihongo|'''Kiso cypress bath'''|木曽檜風呂|Kiso-hinokiburo}}—a wooden soaking tub made from a single ''hinoki'' log that remains the gold standard for onsen bathing in Okaiken.

The [[Kajika]] maritime expansion reached the Kiso coast relatively late, and the interior valleys remained isolated from the coastal trade networks until the development of the [[Kiso Kaidō]] in the 12th century. The Kaidō, a mountain highway that connected the Shendan coast to the Ishijima interior via the Kiso valleys, opened the forest to commercial exploitation and brought the region into the national economy for the first time.

=== The Honda Shogunate and the Kiso Domain ===
The [[Kiso Domain]] ({{lang|ja|木曽藩}}, ''Kiso-han'') was established under the [[Honda Shogunate]] as a retainer territory of the [[Otoge clan]], hereditary vassals of the Honda who controlled the eastern mountain approaches. The domain's wealth was built on the Kiso Forest—the shogunate's demand for ''hinoki'' timber for temple construction and shipbuilding drove the development of a sophisticated forestry industry, and the [[Otoge Forestry Office]], established in 1486, was one of the first government-managed timber operations in Okaiken.

The Shogunate period also saw the emergence of the {{nihongo|'''Kiso Lacquerware School'''|木曽漆器流|Kiso-shikki-ryū}}. The Kiso Forest's abundant ''urushi'' trees provided the raw lacquer, while the forest's ''hinoki'' and ''keyaki'' timber provided the wooden bases. The Kiso style is distinguished by its {{nihongo|"nunobori"|布盛|cloth-laying}} technique, in which layers of hemp cloth are laid over the wooden base before the lacquer is applied, producing a distinctive textured surface that is both extraordinarily durable and visually striking—a contrast to the smooth amber finish of the Tochigi School and the high-gloss black of the Noto School.

=== Modern era ===
The [[Saito Restoration]] of 1871 converted the Kiso Domain into Kiso Prefecture. The new government recognised the economic value of the Kiso Forest and established the [[Kiso Forestry Agency]] in 1873—one year before the Haruno agency—to manage the sustainable harvest of the cypress timber. The agency introduced the {{nihongo|'''Kiso Rotation System'''|木曽輪伐制|Kiso Rinbatsu-sei}}, a 60-year rotation cycle for ''hinoki'' cypress (longer than Haruno's 40-year sugi cycle, reflecting the slower growth rate of the cypress), which is still in use today.

The 20th century saw the Kiso Kaidō modernised into the [[Kiso Expressway]], and the prefecture's traditional craft industries were reorganised for the modern market. The [[Kiso Lacquerware Cooperative]], established in 1932, coordinates production for the approximately 35 active workshops that maintain the Kiso tradition, and the Kiso cypress bath remains a premium product that is exported throughout Okaiken and Fosia.

== Geography ==

Kiso Prefecture occupies the eastern highlands of Ishijima, stretching from the Shendan Ocean coast in the east to the interior mountains that border [[Shimabara Prefecture|Shimabara]] and [[Haruno Prefecture|Haruno]]. The prefecture is one of the most mountainous on Ishijima, with over 65% of its land area covered by the Kiso Cypress Forest and the majority of its population concentrated in the narrow river valleys and coastal terraces.

The {{nihongo|'''Kiso Mountains'''|木曽山脈|Kiso-sanmyaku}} form the spine of the prefecture, rising to 1,400–1,800 m in the interior and creating a dramatic landscape of steep forested slopes, deep river gorges, and high mountain passes. The [[Kiso River]] ({{lang|ja|木曽川}}, ''Kiso-gawa''), the prefecture's longest waterway at 86 km, flows from the interior mountains through the [[Kiso Valley]]—the broad, fertile valley that contains the prefectural capital and the majority of the population—to the Shendan coast.

The [[Kiso Kaidō]] ({{lang|ja|木曽街道}}), the historic mountain highway that traverses the prefecture from north to south, follows the Kiso Valley through a series of mountain passes that have been strategic military and trade corridors since the Sengoku period. The {{nihongo|'''Otoge Pass'''|大峠|Ō-tōge}}, at 1,186 m, is the most important of these routes and the namesake of the [[Otoge, Kiso|Otoge]] municipality.

